V['WORLD']=("Seat W read every citation it could reach in its primary source. That covers Erdős 1950 (pp. 195 and 199–203), Erdős–Graham (pp. 32, 35, 37–38) and the current v2 of van Doorn and Tang (24 May 2026). It also covers Elsholtz, Elsholtz–Planitzer, Martin, Takenouchi, Nakayama, Erdős–Turán, Kumchev, Kumchev–Tolev and Glibichuk–Konyagin. Each says what the paper takes from it, with the hypotheses it needs. Vose 1985 and Tenenbaum–Yokota were checked only through their zbMATH reviews, Konyagin 2014 only through two secondary sources (mathnet.ru was unreachable), and Graham–Kolesnik not at all; the paper reproves what it takes from that book. "
 "Konyagin's own known errata (his equation (4), and a missing \"m odd\" in his Lemma 1, both confirmed by him to Wouter van Doorn per the #148 thread) touch nothing the paper uses. "
 "The paper is dated 25 September; it became public with the release on 6 October (our PDF is byte-identical to the one in openai/math's first commit). "
 "Its history skips two public intermediate results. Liam Price's 24 July proof claim on #18 gives h(n) ≪ (log log n)² for infinitely many practical n. Van Doorn and GPT-6 Astra Pro posted \"Practical numbers and Egyptian fractions\" on 16 September (formalised by Aristotle), with N(b) ≤ 2c<sub>0</sub>(log log b)² and v(k) ≥ exp(exp(√(k/(2c<sub>0</sub>)))), c<sub>0</sub> = 14/log 2. Both are strictly weaker than the paper's theorems, so this is a gap in its exposition, not a priority question. Bloom and van Doorn cite the second. We found no earlier public claim of any of the three double-logarithmic results. "
 "Bloom and van Doorn's exposition flags no mathematical error. It says that the technical arguments of §§3–5, \"occupying 15 pages, out of 33 total\", are \"entirely redundant\", because the paper's own §7–8 method gives Theorem 1.1 more simply, and \"Strangely, nowhere in the paper is this oddity mentioned\". It notes that the lower bound for F(k) follows at once from the one for v(k), since v(k) ≤ kF(k) + 2. It replaces the paper's grouping in §8.2 with base-B digits and the five-product Fourier argument with the Hart–Iosevich sum-product theorem, and gets the length (61/log 2 + o(1))·log log b. Its authors write that they \"have not yet read those sections [3–5] in detail\", so §§3–5 have, as far as we know, no human reading; they rely on the Lean, as this page does for every step it checks only by machine. "
 "On the problems themselves: #304 is answered exactly. #148 and #293 are answered at double-logarithmic precision, which is the order the paper claims, and Erdős and Graham's sharper guesses stay open: their \"c<sub>0</sub><sup>2<sup>n(1−ε)</sup></sup>\" for F, and for v(n) the choice between \"2<sup>2<sup>√n</sup></sup> or 2<sup>2<sup>n(1−ε)</sup></sup>\" (p. 35). The paper's lim inf ≥ log 2/257 rules out the first alternative for v and is consistent with the second. "
 "#293's ambiguity is that the site's wording drops Erdős and Graham's \"least integer v(n) &gt; 1\", which makes the literal reading v(k) = 1. The paper, OEIS A400352 and van Doorn and Tang all use the original definition, and the other natural readings (length at most k; repetitions allowed) get the same order. "
 "In Formal Conjectures, #304's <code>upper_bound</code> is marked research solved with OpenAI's file linked, #148 stays research open, and #293 has no file. Issue #476 is open, and PR #6842 (open since 4 October) adds definitions and certificate checks but no growth theorem. That PR \"rejects the proposed tuple containing 681943 by checking its exact deficit, 131225/8053056\". Its tuple, however, has 144 and 1984 where the forum certificate (14 September) has 44 and 984, apparently \"1/44 + 1/984\" misread. The certificate as posted sums to exactly 1, which seat W found and we re-checked. "
 "The community database (teorth/erdosproblems at 302a00e) still lists all three as open, last updated 31 August 2025.")
